    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/kutztownedu/jobs/5474471/temporary-part-time-instructor-in-pennsylvania-german-studies": {
      "title": "Temporary, Part-Time Instructor in Pennsylvania German Studies",
      "description": "Temporary, Part-Time Instructor in Pennsylvania German Studies Location Kutztown, PA Job Type Temporary Part Time Job Number AY 26.27 0495C Division Academic Affairs Department Society, History and Culture Opening Date 09/08/2026 Closing Date Continuous Bargaining Unit APSCUF Classification Temporary Description Benefits Questions Job Summary Kutztown University of Pennsylvania enrolls approximately 7,500 students across its graduate and undergraduate programs. Located in a charming rural setting in Kutztown, Pennsylvania, the University is just a 20-minute drive from the metropolitan areas of Allentown/Bethlehem and Reading, and about an hour from the Philadelphia metropolitan area. The University seeks to hire employees with experience working with diverse populations. The Department of Society, History, and Culture at Kutztown University invites applications for a 1/4-time temporary position at the Instructor level for the Spring 2027 semester. Candidates must have the ability to teach an introductory course in Pennsylvania German Studies (PGST 010). The part-time temporary position has a regular teaching load of three (3) hours per week. In accordance with the Collective Bargaining Agreement, performance review and evaluation of faculty is based on effective teaching and fulfillment of professional responsibilities; continuing scholarly growth; and service. Invited candidates will be asked to teach a class as part of their on-campus evaluation. Minimum Qualifications - Master's Degree in a relevant field: Arts, Anthropology, German, History, Museum Studies - Ability to teach Pennsylvania German Studies - Successful CV and application review - Demonstrated scholarship about and relationships with the Pennsylvania German Community - Ability to use relevant resources available on Kutztown University campus and in community - A bility to integrate the use of technology into all courses Preferred Qualifications - Ability to effectively work with, teach, and mentor students - Ability to use the Pennsylvania German language - Relevant publications - K nowledge of the Pennsylvania German community and organizations - Fluency in Deitsch Supplemental Information Applicants should submit the following materials through NeoEd: (a) letter of application; (b) a current curriculum vitae; (c) unofficial transcripts; and (d) contact information for at least three (3) references for e-reference requests. Review will begin immediately and conclude on Wednesday, September 30. Successful interviews, reference checks, background clearances (PA Criminal, Child Abuse Clearance & FBI Clearance), and demonstration of ability are requisite qualifications for all positions. The University is very interested in hiring employees who have had extensive experience with diverse populations. Non-Discrimination Statement Kutztown University does not discriminate in employment or educational opportunities on the basis of sex, race, ethnicity, national origin, age, disability, religion, sexual orientation, gender identity, or veteran status. To discuss a complaint of discrimination, please contact the Office of Social Equity, Old Main A-Wing, Room 02, by phone at 610 683-4700 or by email at socialequity@kutztown.edu . Pursuant to Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Kutztown University does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination in any education program or activity that it operates, including admissions and employment. Any inquiries regarding the application of Title IX at Kutztown University may be made to Bradley Davis, the university’s Title IX coordinator, located in Old Main A-Wing, Room 01, by phone at 610-683-4782 or by email at titleix@kutztown.edu . You may also contact the Office for Civil Rights located in the Lyndon Baines Johnson Department of Education Bldg., 400 Maryland Avenue, SW, Washington, DC 20202-1100, by phone at 800-421-3481 (TDD: 800-877-8339), by fax at 202-453-6012, or by